p.src:
The transcript gives the process twice, with a slight variation. Krishnamurti first says visual perception, sensation, contact, and desire. Anderson then recaps it as visual perception, contact, sensation, desire. To avoid false precision, we keep contact and sensation together: $\mathrm{visual\ perception}
\longrightarrow
\mathrm{contact/sensation}
\longrightarrow
D.$ If desire is frustrated, Anderson says, anger follows. Krishnamurti agrees that all the rest follows: $D + \mathrm{frustration}
\longrightarrow
\mathrm{anger\ and\ further\ reaction}.$

